<style>

/*TOC*/

.articleContent {
    position: relative;
   
}

#floating-toc {
    width: 270px;
    position: absolute; /* Default: no top/fixed value */
    right: 3%;
    top: 0;
    background: #ffffff;
    border: 1px solid #e0e0e0;
    border-radius: 8px;
    z-index: 1000;
    font-family: sans-serif;
    box-shadow: 0px 7px 14px 2px #c0c0c0;
}

/* Force Sticky State via JS Class */
#floating-toc.is-sticky {
    position: fixed;
    top: 120px;
    /*right: calc((100% - 1500px) / 2);  */
}

/* Stop at bottom of articleContent */
#floating-toc.is-at-bottom {
    position: absolute;
    top: auto;
    bottom: 0;
}

/* Header Styles */
.toc-header {
    padding: 15px;
    background: #ececec;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;
    border-radius: 8px 8px 0 0;
}

.desktop-toc-title {
    font-weight: bold;
    color: #333;
    display: block;
}
  
 li.toc-h3 {
    padding-left: 30px !important;
}
  li.toc-h3 a {
    padding-left: 5px !important;
    padding-bottom: 0px !important;
    padding-top: 0px !important;
}

#toc-toggle { display: none; }

/* List & Indentation */
#toc-scroll-container {
    max-height: 70vh;
    overflow-y: auto;
}

#toc-list {
    list-style: none !important;
    padding: 10px 0;
    margin: 0;
    width: 95%;
}

#toc-list li a {
    display: block;
    padding: 6px 20px;
    color: #555;
    text-decoration: none;
    font-size: 14px;
    transition: all 0.2s;
}

#toc-list li a:hover {
    background: #f0f7ff;
    color: #007bff;
}
  
  #toc-list li {
    margin-left: 0px;
    list-style-type: none;
    padding-left: 0px;
}

/* H3 Specific Styling (Indented) */
.toc-h3 {
    padding-left: 20px; /* Indent the LI */
}
  
  

.toc-h3 a {
    font-size: 13px !important;
    color: #777 !important;
    border-left: 2px solid #eee;
}
  
  #toc-list li.active {
    border-left: 3px solid red;
    background-color: rgba(255, 0, 0, 0.05); /* Optional: slight highlight */
    transition: all 0.2s ease-in-out;
}

#toc-list li.active a {
    color: red; /* Optional: make the text red too */
    font-weight: bold;
}
  
/* The container that holds the list */
#toc-scroll-container {
    overflow-y: auto;
    transition: max-height 0.4s ease-in-out; /* Smooth transition */
    max-height: calc(80vh - 120px);
}

/* When a wide-container is detected */
#xfloating-toc.toc-shrunken #toc-scroll-container {
    max-height: 25vh;
}

#floating-toc.toc-shrunken.force-expand #toc-scroll-container,
#floating-toc.open #toc-scroll-container {
    max-height: 70vh !important;
}
  
.toc-shrunken .desktop-toc-title {
    display: none;
}

.toc-shrunken #toc-toggle {
    display: block;
    border: none;
}

.toc-shrunken button#toc-toggle span {
    display: none;
}

#floating-toc.toc-shrunken {
    width: 60px;
    height: 57px;
    overflow: hidden;
}





.toc-shrunken.open .desktop-toc-title {
    display: block;
}

.toc-shrunken.open #toc-toggle {
    display: none;
    border: unset;
}

.toc-shrunken.open button#toc-toggle span {
    display: inline;
}

#floating-toc.toc-shrunken.open {
    width: 270px;
    height: auto;
    overflow: unset;
}
  
  #floating-toc.toc-shrunken #toc-scroll-container{
    display:none; 
  }
  
   #floating-toc.toc-shrunken.open #toc-scroll-container{
    display:block; 
  }
  
  .desktop-toc-title{
   cursor:pointer; 
  }
  
  li.toc-h3 {
    margin-bottom: 5px;
    line-height: 1.2em !important;
}
  
@media (max-width: 1440px) {
    .articleContent { padding-right: 0; }
    
    #floating-toc {
        position: fixed !important;
        top: 120px !important;
        left: auto;
        right: 10px;
        width: 60px;
        max-width:300px;
        max-height: 65px;
        overflow: hidden;
        top: 120px !important;
        height: 57px;
        background: #000;
    }

    #floating-toc.open { max-height: 85vh; width: auto !important; }
    #toc-toggle { 
        display: block; 
        width: 100%; 
        border: none; 
        background: none; 
        text-align: left; 
        font-weight: bold;
        cursor: pointer;
    }
    .desktop-toc-title { display: none; }
	
	#floating-toc.open {
        max-height: 85vh;
        width: 300px;
        height: auto;
        background: #fff;
    }
	
	.toc-header {
		background: #000;
		color: #fff;
	}
	
	#floating-toc.open button span {
		display: inline;
	}

	#floating-toc button span {
		display: none;
	}
  
  #toc-scroll-container{
     display:none; 
  }
  
  #floating-toc.open #toc-scroll-container{
     display:block; 
  }
  
  #floating-toc.toc-shrunken #toc-scroll-container {
    max-height: calc(80vh - 120px);
}
  
}
  

  
  @media (max-width: 500px) {
    .articleContent { padding-right: 0; }

    #floating-toc {
        position: fixed !important;
        top: 10px !important;
        left: 10px;
        right: 10px;
        width: auto !important;
        max-width:100%;
        max-height: 65px;
        overflow: hidden;
    }
	
	#floating-toc button span {
		display: inline;
	}
    
  }
</style>
<!--[endheadercontent]-->
<!-- [interactive content] --> <!-- [customHeader] --> <!-- [customTheme] --><div class="roundup-24-custom-theme"><div class="bannerHeaderHolder"><div class="gradient-banner-overlay"><br></div><div class="background-video"><video width="100%" autoplay="autoplay" preload="none" controls="controls" muted="muted" loop="loop"> <source src="https://documents.trendmicro.com/assets/video/00-state-of-AI-security-report-2H--RTnM45G.mp4" type="video/mp4" /> </video></div><div class="banner-content-holder"><div class="banner-content-inner-holder"><h2 class="white" data-toc="0">Fault Lines in the AI Ecosystem</h2><h3 class="white" data-toc="0">TrendAI™ State of AI Security Report</h3><div class="icons-holder-flex"><br></div><div class="textDescription">AI systems were ground zero for cyber risk in the second half of 2025: Critical flaws were on the rise and adversaries targeted every layer of the AI stack. As underlying weaknesses across the ecosystem continue to widen, organizations must adopt a vigilant, AI-first approach to defense.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="full-width-block"><div class="roundup24-banner-section intro-section"><div class="gradient-banner-overlay-content"><br></div><div class="roundup24-content max-wrapper"><p class="white"><strong>By Peter Girnus, Vincenzo Ciancaglini, Morton Swimmer, David Fiser, Alfredo Oliveira, Benjamin Zigh</strong></p><h2 class="white">Key takeaways</h2><ul><li style="color: #fff!important;" data-mce-style="color: #fff!important;">AI-related CVEs have surged, with 2,130 disclosed in 2025 alone — a 34.6% year-over-year increase. Nearly half of scored AI vulnerabilities are categorized as high- or critical-severity, particularly in emerging areas like agentic AI and MCP servers, owing to their complexity and inconsistent security hardening.</li><li style="color: #fff!important;" data-mce-style="color: #fff!important;">A significant number of AI servers are exposed to the internet, run outdated software, and lack proper security controls. This exposes sensitive data and potentially enables unauthorized access to adversaries.</li><li style="color: #fff!important;" data-mce-style="color: #fff!important;">Cybercriminals are using both purpose-built and legitimate AI platforms to drive their operations, including deepfake-based fraud, AI-generated malware, and the exploitation of agentic systems.</li><li style="color: #fff!important;" data-mce-style="color: #fff!important;">Systemic supply chain threats like compromised models, manipulated tokenizers, and unsecure dependencies can lead to operational disruptions that require robust supply chain integrity controls and continuous monitoring to address properly.</li></ul><p class="white"><a href="https://www.trendmicro.com/en_us/what-is/ai.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-mce-href="https://www.trendmicro.com/en_us/what-is/ai.html">AI</a> is <a href="https://www.trendmicro.com/vinfo/us/security/news/threat-landscape/trend-micro-state-of-ai-security-report-1h-2025" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-mce-href="https://www.trendmicro.com/vinfo/us/security/news/threat-landscape/trend-micro-state-of-ai-security-report-1h-2025">reshaping the cybersecurity landscape</a>, introducing both unprecedented opportunities and complex risks.</p><p class="white">TrendAI™ Research’s extensive analysis reveals a rise in AI-related vulnerabilities, with over 6,000 unique cases identified since 2018, culminating in a record-setting surge in 2025. The growth and severity of these vulnerabilities widen the gap between rapid AI adoption and security readiness, creating fertile ground for cyberattacks. Malicious actors continue to exploit weaknesses in AI infrastructure, application layers, and supply chain components, with high and critical vulnerabilities concentrated in emerging areas such as Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers and <a href="https://www.trendmicro.com/en_us/what-is/ai/agentic-ai.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-mce-href="https://www.trendmicro.com/en_us/what-is/ai/agentic-ai.html">agentic AI</a>. The proliferation of exposed and outdated AI deployments further amplifies systemic risk, as organizations race to integrate AI without adequate hardening, monitoring, and update protocols.</p><p class="white">The weaponization and exploitation of AI systems are also becoming a defining feature of modern cyberthreats. Adversaries are turning to AI to generate malware and carry out fraud via deepfakes. Similarly, autonomous agentic AI systems present cybercriminals with the means to perform adaptive, multi-step attacks.</p><p class="white">Taken together, these persistent and converging issues expose the fault lines beneath today’s AI‑first world, underscoring the need for a fundamental shift in how organizations approach security. Static, compliance‑driven measures are no longer sufficient. Security teams must evolve toward adaptive, layered strategies capable of anticipating and mitigating emerging risks. This report outlines those risks in detail and provides guidance for strengthening resilience across an increasingly complex and interconnected AI ecosystem.</p></div></div></div><div class="full-width-block" style="background-color: #000;" data-mce-style="background-color: #000;"><div class="roundup24-banner-section-1 section-banner-image-holder"><div class="roundup24-banner-section bg2-roundup24 "><div class="gradient-banner-overlay-bg"><br></div><div class="background-video bg2"><video width="100%" autoplay="autoplay" preload="none" controls="controls" muted="muted" loop="loop"> <source src="https://documents.trendmicro.com/assets/video/01-state-of-AI-security-report-2H-6HmKPN2.mp4" type="video/mp4" /> </video></div><div class="section-banner-content-holder flex-bottom"><div class="max-wrapper"><h2 class="less-margin-right"><span class="red-highlight"></span>AI vulnerabilities</h2></div></div></div></div></div><div class="full-width-block"><div class="roundup24-banner-section bg3-roundup24"><div class="roundup24-content max-wrapper"><p>The AI ecosystem now poses a significant cybersecurity risk. <strong>TrendAI™ Research’s analysis of 330,239 Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs) identified 6,086 unique vulnerabilities disclosed from 2018 to 2025 that directly affect AI systems</strong>, as broken down in Figure 1.</p><p><img src="https://documents.trendmicro.com/images/uploads/state-of-ai-2h_fig01-bPaeFb5.png" alt="Figure 1. Growth of AI-related vulnerabilities from 2018 to 2025" width="100%" height="100%" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 700px; width: 90%;" data-mce-src="https://documents.trendmicro.com/images/uploads/state-of-ai-2h_fig01-bPaeFb5.png" data-mce-style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 700px; width: 90%;"></p><p class="smallText">Figure 1. Growth of AI-related vulnerabilities from 2018 to 2025</p><p>Notably, <strong>2,130 of these AI-related vulnerabilities were disclosed in 2025 alone</strong>, representing a <strong>34.6% year-over-year increase</strong>. This growth rate nearly doubles the 17.9% increase in overall CVE disclosures, indicating that AI systems are not only becoming more widely deployed but are also attracting increased attention from attackers.</p><p>Key findings:</p><ul><li><strong>Total AI CVEs from 2018 to 2025: <span class="red-highlight">6,086</span></strong> unique vulnerabilities across eight AI subcategories</li><li><strong>AI CVEs in 2025: <span class="red-highlight">2,130</span></strong>, 34.6% year-over-year growth (versus 17.9% overall CVE growth)</li><li><strong>AI share of all CVEs in 2025: <span class="red-highlight">4.42%</span></strong>, the highest annual rate ever recorded</li><li><strong>High/Critical-severity AI CVEs from 2018 to 2025: <span class="red-highlight">1,593</span></strong>, 26.2% of all AI CVEs (CVSS score: 7.0+)</li><li><strong>High/Critical-severity AI CVEs in 2025: <span class="red-highlight">641</span></strong> (517 high-severity and 124 critical-severity vulnerabilities)</li><li><strong>Total CVEs in database: <span class="red-highlight">330,239</span></strong> (48,164 in 2025 alone)</li></ul><h3>The full picture: AI attack surface data</h3><p>The 2025 acceleration (+0.55 percentage points) is fundamentally different from that of previous years, as shown in Table 1, which covers vulnerability data from 2018 to 2025. This growth is broad-based across the large language model (LLM) tools, MCP servers, and traditional machine learning (ML)/GPU categories — not driven by a single vendor audit.</p><div class="tableScroller" style="width: 100%; overflow: hidden; overflow-x: auto;" data-mce-style="width: 100%; overflow: hidden; overflow-x: auto;"><table style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; min-width: 500px;" data-mce-style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; min-width: 500px;" class="mce-item-table"><thead><tr><th><center>Year</center></th><th><center>Total CVEs</center></th><th><center>AI CVEs</center></th><th><center>AI % of total</center></th><th><center>Year-over-year change</center></th><th><center>Notes</center></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2018</td><td>18,672</td><td>178</td><td>0.95%</td><td>–</td><td>Baseline</td></tr><tr><td>2019</td><td>19,200</td><td>201</td><td>1.05%</td><td>0.09</td><td>Early growth</td></tr><tr><td>2020</td><td>19,776</td><td>194</td><td>0.98%</td><td>-0.07</td><td>Covid disruption</td></tr><tr><td>2021</td><td>22,304</td><td>539</td><td>2.42%</td><td>1.44</td><td>TensorFlow audit spike</td></tr><tr><td>2022</td><td>26,491</td><td>569</td><td>2.15%</td><td>-0.27</td><td>Post-spike normalization</td></tr><tr><td>2023</td><td>29,357</td><td>692</td><td>2.36%</td><td>0.21</td><td>Steady growth</td></tr><tr><td>2024</td><td>40,844</td><td>1,583</td><td>3.87%</td><td>1.52</td><td>LLM infrastructure emerges</td></tr><tr><td>2025</td><td>48,164</td><td>2,130</td><td>4.42%</td><td>0.55</td><td>Sustained acceleration</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="smallText">Table 1. Trends in AI vulnerability disclosures and growth rates from 2018 to 2025</p><h3>Subcategories: Not all AI vulnerabilities are equal</h3><p>AI vulnerabilities cluster into eight distinct subcategories, which are outlined below. The subcategories are not mutually exclusive; a single CVE may be classified into multiple subcategories.</p><ul><li><strong>GPU and AI hardware </strong><span class="red-highlight">(3,127 CVEs, 51.4%)</span>: Encompasses Nvidia drivers, CUDA libraries, vGPU software, and DGX platforms. Primarily memory corruption issues enabling privilege escalation and container escape.</li><li><strong>ML frameworks </strong><span class="red-highlight">(1,624 CVEs, 26.7%)</span>: Includes TensorFlow, PyTorch, MLflow. Primary risk is supply chain compromise through malicious model files that execute embedded code.</li><li><strong>LLM tools and applications </strong><span class="red-highlight">(1,243 CVEs, 20.4%)</span>: Reflects explosive post-ChatGPT growth. Langflow, vLLM, Dify, AnythingLLM deployed without mature security. Code injection, SSRF, and path traversal dominate.</li><li><strong>AI model security </strong><span class="red-highlight">(876 CVEs, 14.4%)</span>: Covers models serving infrastructure, inference engines, and validation systems. Enables model theft, adversarial manipulation, and unauthorized access.</li><li><strong>AI data pipelines </strong><span class="red-highlight">(755 CVEs, 12.4%)</span>: Encompasses data ingestion, preprocessing, and feature engineering. Risks data poisoning, training data exfiltration, and ML lifecycle corruption.</li><li><strong>Agentic AI </strong><span class="red-highlight">(363 CVEs, 6.0%)</span>: Covers vulnerabilities in autonomous AI systems. Flaws in guardrails and execution boundaries create new attack vectors as agents interact with external systems.</li><li><strong>AI supply chain </strong><span class="red-highlight">(114 CVEs, 1.9%)</span>: Includes package managers, model registries, and dependency management. Enables supply chain attacks through compromised models and malicious packages.</li><li><strong>MCP servers </strong><span class="red-highlight">(102 CVEs, 1.7%)</span>: An emerging subcategory, accounting for 95 of 102 CVEs discovered in 2025. MCP creates command injection risks by design.</li></ul><p>Note: Because the subcategories can overlap, their counts add up to 8,204 CVEs, while the dataset contains 6,086 unique CVEs. All percentages are based on the 6,086-CVE total.</p><h3>Severity distribution: Where the real risk lies</h3><p>As shown in Table 2, the severity distribution of AI‑related vulnerabilities shows that nearly half of all scored AI CVEs fall into the high‑ or critical‑severity range, underscoring the significant security risks present across the ecosystem. Of the 6,086 total AI vulnerabilities recorded, 3,257 have assigned CVSS scores, with 11.1% classified as critical (CVSS score: 9.0+) and 37.9% categorized as high-severity (CVSS score: 7.0–8.9). In total, high‑ and critical‑severity CVEs comprise 1,593 cases, representing 48.9% of scored vulnerabilities and 26.2% of all AI CVEs.</p><div class="tableScroller" style="width: 100%; overflow: hidden; overflow-x: auto;" data-mce-style="width: 100%; overflow: hidden; overflow-x: auto;"><table style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; min-width: 500px;" data-mce-style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; min-width: 500px;" class="mce-item-table"><thead><tr><th><center>Severity</center></th><th><center>Count</center></th><th><center>% of scored</center></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Critical (9.0+)</td><td>360</td><td>11.1%</td></tr><tr><td>High (7.0–8.9)</td><td>1,233</td><td>37.9%</td></tr><tr><td>Medium (4.0–6.9)</td><td>1,397</td><td>42.9%</td></tr><tr><td>Low (0.1–3.9)</td><td>267</td><td>8.2%</td></tr><tr><td>Unscored</td><td>2,829</td><td>N/A</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="smallText">Table 2. Severity distribution across AI vulnerabilities from 2018 to 2025</p><p>Figure 2 indicates that high‑ and critical‑severity vulnerabilities are concentrated in the newest and fastest‑growing parts of the AI ecosystem. The AI supply chain (46.5%) and LLM ecosystem (45.1%) show the highest severity levels, followed by agentic AI (40.5%) and AI model security (40.3%). MCP servers also exhibit a substantial share (38.2%). In contrast, mature categories such as ML frameworks (32.9%), AI data pipelines (27%), and GPU/AI hardware (18%) show lower severity concentrations. Overall, emerging AI components carry disproportionately higher risk due to rapid adoption, complex integrations, and inconsistent security hardening.</p><p><img src="https://documents.trendmicro.com/images/uploads/state-of-ai-2h_fig02-Qlra70p.png" alt="Figure 2.  Severity distribution (high/critical concentration) across AI vulnerability subcategories from 2018 to 2025" width="100%" height="100%" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 700px; width: 90%;" data-mce-src="https://documents.trendmicro.com/images/uploads/state-of-ai-2h_fig02-Qlra70p.png" data-mce-style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 700px; width: 90%;"></p><p class="smallText">Figure 2. Severity distribution (high/critical concentration) across AI vulnerability subcategories from 2018 to 2025</p><h3>Accelerating risk: AI attack surface evolution</h3><p>Based on the data visualized in Figure 3 and presented in Table 3, the AI vulnerability landscape has evolved through three distinct phases:</p><ul><li><strong>Phase 1 (2018-2020):</strong> Vulnerabilities in the GPU and hardware subcategory dominated. Volumes were modest, with minimal high- or critical-severity CVEs.</li><li><strong>Phase 2 (2021-2023):</strong> Vulnerabilities in the ML frameworks subcategory took precedence, driven by Google’s systematic TensorFlow security audits.</li><li><strong>Phase 3 (2024-2025):</strong> Vulnerabilities in the LLM tools and applications subcategory now dominate. 80.4% year-over-year growth in CVEs under the LLM ecosystem subcategory (419 → 756). 2025 shows 641 high/critical-severity CVEs, up from 484 in 2024.</li></ul><p><strong>Our critical finding:</strong> The shift from infrastructure to application-layer vulnerabilities indicates attackers are following AI adoption into production.</p><p><img src="https://documents.trendmicro.com/images/uploads/state-of-ai-2h_fig03-QChMVTd.png" alt="Figure 3. Distribution and growth of high- and critical-severity AI CVEs from 2018 to 2025" width="100%" height="100%" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 700px; width: 90%;" data-mce-src="https://documents.trendmicro.com/images/uploads/state-of-ai-2h_fig03-QChMVTd.png" data-mce-style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 700px; width: 90%;"></p><p class="smallText">Figure 3. Distribution and growth of high- and critical-severity AI CVEs from 2018 to 2025</p><div class="tableScroller" style="width: 100%; overflow: hidden; overflow-x: auto;" data-mce-style="width: 100%; overflow: hidden; overflow-x: auto;"><table style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; min-width: 500px;" data-mce-style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; min-width: 500px;" class="mce-item-table"><thead><tr><th><center>Year</center></th><th><center>Critical (9.0+)</center></th><th><center>High (7.0–8.9)</center></th><th><center>Total high and critical</center></th><th><center>Year-over-year growth</center></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2018</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td>2019</td><td>0</td><td>3</td><td>3</td><td>200%</td></tr><tr><td>2020</td><td>6</td><td>14</td><td>20</td><td>567%</td></tr><tr><td>2021</td><td>12</td><td>113</td><td>125</td><td>525%</td></tr><tr><td>2022</td><td>37</td><td>97</td><td>134</td><td>7%</td></tr><tr><td>2023</td><td>40</td><td>145</td><td>185</td><td>38%</td></tr><tr><td>2024</td><td>141</td><td>343</td><td>484</td><td>162%</td></tr><tr><td>2025</td><td>124</td><td>517</td><td>641</td><td>32%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="smallText">Table 3. Distribution and growth of high- and critical-severity AI vulnerabilities from 2018 to 2025</p><p>The 2021 spike was artificial, as it was caused by a single vendor audit. But the year-over-year growth of AI vulnerabilities from 2024 to 2025, presented in Figure 4, shows a clear acceleration driven by structural rather than incidental factors. Agentic AI saw the largest jump, rising from 74 to 263 CVEs (+255.4%), followed by the AI supply chain, which doubled from 26 to 52, and the LLM ecosystem, which grew from 419 to 756 (+80.4%). Core categories such as AI model security (+76.1%) and ML frameworks (+52.2%) also expanded significantly, while GPU and AI hardware increased by 21.6%. In contrast, AI data pipelines declined by 11.4%, and MCP servers emerged as a new attack surface with 95 CVEs after having none in 2024. Collectively, these shifts reflect a rapidly widening AI attack surface fueled by growing deployment complexity and the fast adoption of agentic and LLM-based systems.</p><p><img src="https://documents.trendmicro.com/images/uploads/state-of-ai-2h_fig04-KqaRNPF.png" alt="Figure 4. Year-over-year growth of AI vulnerabilities by subcategory from 2024 to 2025" width="100%" height="100%" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 700px; width: 90%;" data-mce-src="https://documents.trendmicro.com/images/uploads/state-of-ai-2h_fig04-KqaRNPF.png" data-mce-style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 700px; width: 90%;"></p><p class="smallText">Figure 4. Year-over-year growth of AI vulnerabilities by subcategory from 2024 to 2025</p><p>As visualized in Figure 5, the evolution of the AI attack surface from 2020 to 2025 shows rapid growth across key subcategories, with especially sharp increases beginning in 2024. GPU and AI hardware and ML frameworks expanded steadily throughout the period, while the LLM ecosystem surged from 419 to 756 CVEs in 2025 and MCP servers emerged suddenly with 95 new cases in 2025 after having none in prior years. Together, these trends reflect how the attack surface is widening as key AI subcategories grow more complex and widely deployed.</p><p><img src="https://documents.trendmicro.com/images/uploads/state-of-ai-2h_fig05-q4n9ytf.png" alt="Figure 5. Growth of AI vulnerabilities across key subcategories from 2020 to 2025" width="100%" height="100%" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 700px; width: 90%;" data-mce-src="https://documents.trendmicro.com/images/uploads/state-of-ai-2h_fig05-q4n9ytf.png" data-mce-style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 700px; width: 90%;"></p><p class="smallText">Figure 5. Growth of AI vulnerabilities across key subcategories from 2020 to 2025</p><!--div class="tableScroller" style="width: 100%; overflow: hidden; overflow-x: auto;">
